              "type": "text",
              "text": "Now let me identify the explanatory em-dashes. I see three `---` instances:\n\n1. Line 2: `93\\% prove rate---yet 87\\%` — This is a contrast/pivot, not explanatory. \"yet\" signals adversative relation. This is genuinely appropriate as a dramatic pause before a contrasting reveal.\n\n2. Line 7: `dataset-dependent outcomes---Goodhart collapse on FOLIO versus beneficial instability on ProofFOL` — The second clause explains/elaborates what \"dataset-dependent outcomes\" means. This is an explanatory em-dash.\n\n3. Line 8: `8 hacking modes---documenting ISR's asymmetry` — The second clause elaborates on the significance of cataloging 8 hacking modes. This is an explanatory em-dash.\n\nLooking more carefully at line 2: `93\\% prove rate---yet 87\\%` — while \"yet\" makes it adversative, the em-dash before \"yet\" is a common AI writing pattern. A semicolon would be more natural for connecting two independent clauses with an adversative conjunction. Let me reconsider — actually, the dash here creates a dramatic reveal effect that is stylistically appropriate in an abstract. But the user asked me to find ~3 explanatory ones where the second clause explains/elaborates. Line 2 is adversative, not explanatory.\n\nLet me re-examine. There are only 3 em-dashes total (lines 2, 7, 8). The user says ~3 are explanatory. Let me reconsider line 2: the structure is \"achieves X---yet Y\". The \"yet\" clause reveals that the high prove rate is degenerate. It's more of a contrast than an explanation. But it is an AI-style dramatic em-dash that could be a semicolon.\n\nI'll treat all three as candidates for replacement since the user expects ~3:\n\n- Line 2: contrast → semicolon works well with \"yet\"\n- Line 7: elaboration → colon (specifying what \"dataset-dependent outcomes\" are)\n- Line 8: elaboration → comma or colon"
